[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce any routing or inter-VLAN outage. The OSPF domain and interconnects are healthy, and all user VLANs are consistently advertised and reachable.

Evidence:
- Topology-wide probes show 0% loss between groups: from a VLAN 5 host (pc1, 10.5.0.1) to VLANs 10/20/30 and VLAN 6 all succeeded, as did reverse tests from VLANs 10/20/30 and VLAN 6 back toward VLAN 5 (e.g., pc10→dc_pc5, pc12→dc_pc5, pc9→pc4).
- On all core routers (router7, router8, router10, router11, router16, router17), “show ip route” contains OSPF routes to every user subnet (172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24, 172.16.30.0/24, 10.5.0.0/24, 10.6.0.0/24) with sane next-hops and metrics. No route is missing or mispointed.

Mechanism and localization: If an entire group had “disappeared” due to routing, we would see missing OSPF entries and consistent cross-subnet ping failures from multiple sources. Instead, reachability works end-to-end across the fabric and through router7↔router16 toward the access switches. This isolates the issue away from the routers/switching plane.

Root cause: Not a network-side routing problem; the behavior is consistent with a transient or endpoint-local issue (e.g., stale ARP/DNS cache, host firewall, or incorrect gateway on the reporter’s PC).

Corrective fix: No network changes required. On the affected PC, verify IP/gateway, clear local caches (flush DNS; clear ARP), and check host firewall rules for that destination subnet. If it recurs, capture ARP/ICMP from the PC to the target group to pinpoint the host-side cause. 
=== END ===